== 7. Of Rejection, or Refusal of the Contract

Contrary to a complete and lawful contract#footnote[Repudium.] is rejection, by which the contract is dissolved, or broken off.

A lawful contract is dissolved when some great and heinous fault follows immediately upon it in either of the betrothed parties. And from this arise various cases to be set down and resolved.

The first is this. What is to be done when some disease comes upon one of the parties immediately after the contract is made?

#emph[Answer.] Those diseases that take away the use of the body, and altogether disable the party from performing the promise made with respect to marriage duties, are very just impediments to marriage, and so break off the contract. Of this sort are incurable palsies, frigidity, and the like, of which I have spoken before.

Nevertheless, a covenant once made is not to be renounced suddenly, for fear of giving offense. And therefore some longer respite is to be taken from the time of the contract, in which they are to wait in expectation of recovery, before the said diseases are publicly and solemnly declared incurable, and so the covenant made is declared void.

Now, the disease may not take away the use of the body for the present, and yet in time prove incurably contagious, and so loathsome that the one may justly fear to keep company and live with the other, as happens when one is infected with leprosy. Then the contract is to be utterly dissolved, as if God himself had commanded it, though the promise was made before, and though the parties themselves should be unwilling. God has ordained matrimony to help, not to hurt, either the persons themselves or others. Where, therefore, there are such diseases as may infect, hurt, or destroy others, there God has, as it were, testified from heaven that the act done does not please him, and that it ought at once to be made void.

The contract being once dissolved in this way, the sounder party will be at liberty to marry again. But the diseased party is, by the magistrate’s authority, to be forbidden union with any other by way of marriage, and commanded to lead his life where he may fittingly live apart from company, for fear of infection. And for his own part he is also to seek from God, by prayer in faith, the gift of continency. For it is certain that he to whom God has denied the power of using marriage with a good conscience is by that very fact called to continency and single life.

But suppose the disease is not contagious, yet is either incurable or so foul that either of the betrothed has just cause to be afraid of the other’s company. Then both are to be advised not to claim their right, but willingly to surrender it to each other by mutual consent, so that they may be free again, and not bound by any former promise to live together as man and wife. For they have no hope of living together with comfort and contentment while they remain so.

Upon this advice, given by their lawful pastor or someone in his stead, if they both agree, or at least one of them desires to be at liberty, the espousals are to be broken off at once. But if neither will consent to a separation, but they would rather proceed as they have begun, and in all probability the one has no just cause to hold the other in suspicion in respect of contagion, they are not to be hindered from completing their marriage.

Furthermore, one of them may have a disease that is continual, without intermission, and yet curable; or some other misfortune may come upon either of them after the sure-making, by which they become lame, deaf, or mute, etc. Yet this is not a sufficient cause to move them to renounce and dissolve the contract.

A second case. What if it happens that after the contract is finished, one of the betrothed persons is absent from the other for a long time, so that the absence is prejudicial to the marriage that should follow on the contract?

#emph[Answer.] Inquiry must be made whether this absence is voluntary, or against the will of the party.

Suppose it is voluntary. For example, the man, on an honorable and just cause, the woman also knowing of it, goes overseas; and having been admonished of his duty beforehand, he nevertheless stays a whole year or more and will not return, but wilfully absents himself further. Then it may be lawful for the woman to ask the public magistrate to dissolve the covenant made, and so to marry another man, unless the former man, to whom she was contracted, is heard of. If he is, then it will be lawful for him to plead for himself; and if he offers no just cause for his refusal, he will be compelled by the magistrate’s authority to stand by his promise and take the woman as his wife. And the same course is to be taken in the wilful absence of the woman after she is contracted.

Again, the man or the woman may absent himself or herself and depart with an evil mind, and with the purpose of deceiving the other. Then means must be used to have the party admonished of his or her duty. When that has been done, or at least attempted, and the offending party still will not appear, if the other who is present, certainly knowing his or her own lack of continency, appeals to the magistrate, then after public and solemn notice is given the contract may be broken.

But on the other hand, either of them may be absent against his will, intending no fraud or deceit, but forcibly detained by captivity, imprisonment, sickness, force, banishment, transportation, or the like. Then the party present, if desirous of completing the marriage, ought either to make efforts to reach him and wait for his return, or to have certain notice of his death, before she promises herself to anyone else, or has to do with any other by way of marriage.

A third case. What if after the contract one of the parties becomes raving or mad?

#emph[Answer.] If the disease is continual, without intermission, then in all equity and likelihood the espousals are to be dissolved, unless both parties, after knowing and experiencing the disease, have known one another. Yet in this and all such cases it is fitting that all good means be tried and used, and sufficient time be given, for restoring the diseased to their former good condition, and for completing the marriage lawfully begun, as before.
